Q: Dynamic dispatching of template functions? A: <p>Here is a proposal that &quot;converts&quot; an integer whose value is known at runtime into an integer that can be used as non-type template argument. It works starting with c++17 though since
it depends on <code>std::variant</code></p>
<p>The idea is to rely on a variant whose types hold compile time constants of type <code>std::integral_constant</code>. For instance,
one could have:</p>
<pre><code>int main (int argc, char** argv)
{
    std::variant &lt;
        std::integral_constant&lt;std::size_t,0&gt;,
        std::integral_constant&lt;std::size_t,1&gt;,
        std::integral_constant&lt;std::size_t,2&gt;
    &gt; v;

    v = std::integral_constant&lt;std::size_t,2&gt;{};
    std::visit ([](auto idx)  { A&lt;decltype(idx)::value&gt;::foo();  }, v);

    v = std::integral_constant&lt;std::size_t,1&gt;{};
    std::visit ([](auto idx)  { A&lt;decltype(idx)::value&gt;::foo();  }, v);
}
</code></pre>
<p>where we use <code>std::visit</code> on a variant whose value may change at runtime. Note how the <code>idx</code> parameter
of the lambda provided to <code>std::visit</code> holds our compile time information in the <code>std::integral_constant</code> type.</p>
<p>We can encapsulate this idea in a <code>as_integral_constant_variant</code> function that can be used as follows:</p>
<pre><code>int main (int argc, char** argv)
{
    int n = argc&gt;=2 ? std::atoi(argv[1]) : 0;

    // We can retrieve type information from 'decltype(idx)' and use it as a NTTP
    std::visit ([&amp;](auto i)  {
        A&lt;decltype(i)::value&gt;::foo();
    }, as_integral_constant_variant(n));
}
</code></pre>
<p>Now, for the definition of <code>as_integral_constant_variant</code>. First, it takes a non-type template parameter <code>K</code> (with a possible default value); it defines which integers from 0 to <code>K-1</code> can be managed.</p>
<p>We also need to define our variant <code>integral_constant_variant</code> type with <code>K</code> types of <code>std::integral_constant</code>, from <code>0</code> to <code>K-1</code>. We can use
some metaprogramming here:</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;std::size_t...Is&gt;
static auto buildtype (std::index_sequence&lt;Is...&gt;)
    -&gt; std::variant &lt;std::integral_constant&lt;int,Is&gt;...&gt;;

template&lt;int Kmax&gt;
using integral_constant_variant = decltype (buildtype (std::make_index_sequence&lt;Kmax&gt;{}));
</code></pre>
<p>Finally, we define a <code>select</code> function that returns an instance of our variant initialized with the correct object according to the input integer value <code>n</code>:</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;int Kmax, int K=Kmax, int Remaining=0&gt;
constexpr auto select (std::size_t n) -&gt; std::optional&lt;integral_constant_variant&lt;Kmax&gt;&gt; {
    if constexpr (K &gt; 0) {
        if (n==0)  {  return integral_constant_variant&lt;Kmax&gt; {std::integral_constant&lt;int,Remaining&gt;{}};  }
        else       {  return select &lt;Kmax, K-1,Remaining+1&gt; (n-1);  }
    }
    else  {  return {};  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Note that we actually use a <code>std::optional</code> type here; it is used to track the case when the input integer value can't be associated to one of the <code>std::integral_constant</code> types of the variant.</p>
<p>Here is a full example:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;variant&gt;
#include &lt;optional&gt;
#include &lt;iostream&gt;

namespace details {

template&lt;std::size_t...Is&gt;
static auto buildtype (std::index_sequence&lt;Is...&gt;)
    -&gt; std::variant &lt;std::integral_constant&lt;int,Is&gt;...&gt;;

template&lt;int Kmax&gt;
using integral_constant_variant = decltype (buildtype (std::make_index_sequence&lt;Kmax&gt;{}));

    template&lt;int Kmax, int K=Kmax, int Remaining=0&gt;
    constexpr auto select (std::size_t n) -&gt; std::optional&lt;integral_constant_variant&lt;Kmax&gt;&gt; {
        if constexpr (K &gt; 0) {
            if (n==0)  {  return integral_constant_variant&lt;Kmax&gt; {std::integral_constant&lt;int,Remaining&gt;{}};  }
            else       {  return select &lt;Kmax, K-1,Remaining+1&gt; (n-1);  }
        }
        else  {  return {};  }
    }
}

template&lt;int K=10&gt;
auto as_integral_constant_variant (std::size_t n) {
    // We might have an exception here in case the optional object has no value.
    // This would happen if the integer value 'n' is not in the integer range
    // allowed by the 'select' function.
    return details::select&lt;K&gt;(n).value();
}

///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

template&lt;std::size_t I&gt;
struct A {
    static void foo() {  std::cout &lt;&lt; I &lt;&lt; &quot;\n&quot;;  }
};

int main (int argc, char** argv)
{
    int n = argc&gt;=2 ? std::atoi(argv[1]) : 0;

    std::visit ([&amp;](auto i)  {
        A&lt;decltype(i)::value&gt;::foo();
    }, as_integral_constant_variant(n));
}
</code></pre>
